From 33a9c6dac1d27a4cb0cb303687464c3d4e7b6633 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: snarayana <snarayana@student.ethz.ch>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:37:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(protocol): republish our own row when it holds an
 earlier run's record

A restarted node keeps its peer ID but not its CRDT store (cleanslate
defaults to true, and shutdown wipes the store too). The mesh still holds
the previous run's row: its LEFT record, a head's eviction record, or the
services it advertised before a crash. The restarted node's first writes
lose to that row, and once the node has caught up nothing writes again
unless a new connection, a relay change or POST /v1/dnt/_node happens to
trigger it. Until then the head's /v1/dnt/table omits the worker while the
worker lists itself as ready (#73). The two-minute reannounce that used to
cover this went with the DAG-bloat fix in a8c1055.

Stamp every record we publish with a per-process incarnation, in
applyLocalRuntimeMetadata, which every self-row writer already calls.
When the PutHook sees our own row holding a record from another
incarnation, republish once, after a 1 s debounce. With the previous
commit the republish outranks the record it answers, so the row converges
within one broadcast instead of waiting for the history walk. Records
from this run are left alone, including a head's eviction of this run,
which copies our record and its incarnation, so the guard does not fight
a live eviction. The guard is off after AnnounceLeave.

There are no periodic writes: a restart costs one extra write.

Refs #73

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
